---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TopologyDiagram from "../components/TopologyDiagram.astro";
import CardFilter from "../components/CardFilter.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import { profile, stats } from "../data/profile";

const cardEntries = (await getCollection("cards")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const uniqueTags = [...new Set(cardEntries.flatMap((entry) => entry.data.tags))].sort();

const rendered = await Promise.all(
  cardEntries.map(async (entry) => ({ entry, Content: (await entry.render()).Content }))
);
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${profile.name} — ${profile.jobTitle}`}
  description={`I design, build and operate ${profile.project}, a live 3-player multiplayer game, alone — and automate the team I don't have. Six engineering trade-offs, the option I turned down each time, and the code that proves it.`}
>
  <header class="site-header">
    <a class="wordmark" href="/">
      <span class="wordmark-mark" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      {profile.name}
    </a>
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="#tradeoffs">Trade-offs</a>
      <a href="#work">Work with me</a>
      <a href={profile.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Code</a>
      <a class="nav-cta" href="#contact">Contact</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main id="main">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">{profile.title}</p>
      <h1>
        I automate the team I don't have —<br class="hero-break" />
        so I can run production alone.
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        <strong>{profile.project} ({profile.projectShort})</strong> is a live three-player
        game people play over the internet, on Android and in the browser. I designed it,
        built it and operate it end to end — the servers it runs on, the game logic, the
        client, and the pipeline that ships all of it. Coding agents do a lot of the
        implementing; I hold the requirement, the review and the sign-off.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        Underneath: a Godot client, a server that holds the only authoritative copy of the
        game, and three deployment targets built from one container image — EKS for
        production-grade infrastructure, a K3s box as the lab, and a machine at home as the
        cost-efficient development target.
      </p>

      <dl class="stats">
        {stats.map((stat) => (
          <div class="stat">
            <dt>
              <span class="stat-value">{stat.value}</span>
              <span class="stat-label">{stat.label}</span>
            </dt>
            <dd>{stat.sub}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <p class="stack-line">
        Terraform · Kubernetes (EKS / K3s) · AWS · Docker · GitHub Actions · Cloudflare ·
        Node.js · Redis · GDScript / Godot · Ansible
      </p>

      <div class="ctas">
        <a class="cta primary" href={profile.demo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Play the live demo
        </a>
        <a class="cta secondary" href={profile.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          Read the source
        </a>
        <a class="cta ghost" href="#tradeoffs">Skip to the trade-offs</a>
      </div>
      <p class="disclosure">
        Two things to know before you click. The demo fills empty seats with bots so a lone
        visitor still gets a full round — the real game is three human players. And it is
        served from the machine at home, not from EKS, because that box is the
        cost-efficient target rather than the production-grade one: expect higher latency
        than the production stack would give you. That trade is deliberate, and it's the
        first trade-off below.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="diagram-section" aria-labelledby="topology-heading">
      <h2 id="topology-heading" class="section-title">
        <span class="section-index">01</span> One image, three targets
      </h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        The same container image runs in all three. What differs is only what has to
        differ — and each of those differences was a decision with a rejected
        alternative.
      </p>
      <div class="diagram-wrap">
        <TopologyDiagram />
      </div>
      <p class="scroll-hint">Scroll the diagram sideways →</p>
      <p class="figure-caption">
        <strong>EKS is the production-grade target</strong> — load balancer, managed nodes
        in private subnets, managed Redis — stood up for a session and destroyed after it,
        so it costs nothing while nobody is using it. <strong>K3s on one EC2 instance is
        the lab</strong>, where changes get tested and where I learn the parts I haven't
        run before. <strong>One Linux box at home is the cost-efficient development
        target</strong>, behind a Cloudflare Tunnel with no managed services at all — and
        it's what serves the public demo.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="tradeoffs" aria-labelledby="tradeoffs-heading">
      <h2 id="tradeoffs-heading" class="section-title">
        <span class="section-index">02</span> Six trade-offs, and what I turned down
      </h2>
      <p class="section-intro">
        Every card is one trade-off: what I chose, the option I rejected, and a link to the
        code that proves it — not a list of technologies, the reasoning underneath them.
        Each opens with the plain-English version; expand it for the engineering rationale.
        The topics cut across all six — filter to see where one concern keeps showing up.
      </p>

      <CardFilter tags={uniqueTags} total={cardEntries.length} />

      <div class="cards">
        {rendered.map(({ entry, Content }) => (
          <Card
            role={entry.data.role}
            headline={entry.data.headline}
            plain={entry.data.plain}
            metric={entry.data.metric}
            metricLabel={entry.data.metricLabel}
            tags={entry.data.tags}
            links={entry.data.links}
          >
            <Content />
          </Card>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="work" aria-labelledby="work-heading">
      <h2 id="work-heading" class="section-title">
        <span class="section-index">03</span> Work with me
      </h2>
      <div class="services">
        <article class="service">
          <h3>Cloud &amp; platform engineering</h3>
          <p>
            Terraform roots, Kubernetes on EC2 or EKS, private networking, TLS and DNS,
            cost bounded by design rather than by an alert that arrives a day late.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="service">
          <h3>Delivery automation &amp; guardrails</h3>
          <p>
            Release pipelines that verify their own output, drift checks that fail before
            a build starts, and destructive operations that require a typed confirmation
            naming the exact target.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="service">
          <h3>AI-assisted delivery, done properly</h3>
          <p>
            Agents in the implementer's seat with written acceptance criteria, a rejection
            path, and CI gates cleared before a human looks. Plus the retrieval layer that
            lets an agent work in your codebase without re-reading it every session.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="how-i-work">
        <h3>How I work with coding agents</h3>
        <p>
          I treat an agent the way an organisation treats a contractor: the requirement is
          written down first, the work is tested against it, and it comes back with
          specifics until nothing is outstanding. Every language in this repository is one
          I read fluently — Node.js on the server, GDScript in the client — because when
          the agent stalls, the work has to continue. That's a deliberate constraint, not
          an accident of what was fastest to generate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="contact-heading">
      <h2 id="contact-heading" class="section-title">
        <span class="section-index">04</span> Get in touch
      </h2>
      {profile.availability && <p class="availability">{profile.availability}</p>}
      <div class="contact-links">
        <a class="cta primary" href={`mailto:${profile.email}`}>{profile.email}</a>
        {profile.linkedin && (
          <a class="cta secondary" href={profile.linkedin} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        )}
        <a class="cta secondary" href={profile.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          GitHub
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>
    <p>
      <strong>{profile.name}</strong> — {profile.title}
      {profile.location && <> · {profile.location}</>}
    </p>
    <p>
      Every claim on this page links to the file, workflow or test that backs it, in
      <a href={profile.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the public repository</a>
      — which still carries the project's working name, Corp Tower. This site is a static
      Astro build deployed to Cloudflare Workers on every push.
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
